---
import "../../styles/global.css";
import "@fontsource/instrument-serif";
import "@fontsource-variable/noto-serif-kr";
import "@fontsource-variable/instrument-sans";
import "@fontsource/im-fell-great-primer/400-italic.css";
import "@fontsource/im-fell-great-primer";
import Navbar from "../../components/Navbar.astro";
---

<meta charset="UTF-8" />
<head>
</head>
<body class="bg-offwhite">
    <Navbar />
    <main class="ml-20 mr-20 bg-offwhite">
        <div>
            <div class="im-fell-italic bold text-6xl pt-10">
                Track the Crowd
            </div>
            <div class="text-2xl pt-4 im-fell-italic">
                Tools used: Arduino, 3D Printing (Fusion + Bambu Lab), Python (YOLOv8)
            </div>
            <div class="text-xl pt-4 pb-4 im-fell">
                CSC490: Physical Computing for Interactive Experiences
            </div>
            
        </div>
        <div class="w-1/2">
            <div class="text-xl pt-4 pb-4 im-fell">
            This semester, I had the opportunity to work with Joshua Li, Noelle Ransom, and Maksym Woychyshyn on our Capstone Design Project, focused on physical computing for interactive experiences in social good.
            </div>
            <div class="text-xl pt-4 pb-4 im-fell">
                Our project aimed to reduce subway congestion by installing a camera at the platform level to monitor how full each subway car is as it departs. 
            </div>
            <div class="text-xl pt-4 pb-4 im-fell">
                Using an image recognition model YOLOv8, we detect crowd density in each car and communicate this in real-time to passengers via an Arduino-powered LED panel.
            </div>
        </div>
    </main>
</body>
